\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{Two budget conventions.}{37}{section*.37}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces A fixed wall-clock budget locks the cost of a single evaluation and the number of evaluations completed onto a hyperbola; the grey line is the $90$-second cap shared by every configuration. Sitting on the line means cost $\times $ count equals that configuration's wall-clock time. The two closed-loop configurations sit on the line---every run uses the full budget; the open loop sits \emph  {below} the line, because it stops of its own after a little over four hundred generations without improvement, converging in about $17$ seconds, and is not cut off by the budget (Section\nonbreakingspace \ref {sec:protocol}). B0 and B0$^+$ share the same open-loop search and therefore share a point; they differ only in how they are executed. Pale small points are the per-instance readings of three instances; the filled large point is their geometric mean: on a log axis only the geometric mean preserves ``cost $\times $ count $=$ that configuration's wall-clock time''. Orange markers are the on/off pair of pruning and winner-path reuse (the callout marks the on setting)---Proposition\nonbreakingspace \ref {prop:speedup_equiv} guarantees that one can move along that line \emph  {without changing the output}, so this displacement is only a saving of compute.}}{38}{figure.caption.38}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:protocol}{{6}{38}{A fixed wall-clock budget locks the cost of a single evaluation and the number of evaluations completed onto a hyperbola; the grey line is the $90$-second cap shared by every configuration. Sitting on the line means cost $\times $ count equals that configuration's wall-clock time. The two closed-loop configurations sit on the line---every run uses the full budget; the open loop sits \emph {below} the line, because it stops of its own after a little over four hundred generations without improvement, converging in about $17$ seconds, and is not cut off by the budget (Section~\ref {sec:protocol}). B0 and B0$^+$ share the same open-loop search and therefore share a point; they differ only in how they are executed. Pale small points are the per-instance readings of three instances; the filled large point is their geometric mean: on a log axis only the geometric mean preserves ``cost $\times $ count $=$ that configuration's wall-clock time''. Orange markers are the on/off pair of pruning and winner-path reuse (the callout marks the on setting)---Proposition~\ref {prop:speedup_equiv} guarantees that one can move along that line \emph {without changing the output}, so this displacement is only a saving of compute}{figure.caption.38}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{The cost law.}{38}{section*.40}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces The same set of comparisons under two budget conventions. Each row is named by the mechanism it isolates, not by a pair of configurations. The difference between the two markers comes from the convention: reservation-table-aware dispatching is 2.90\%{} under equal generation count ($p=0.0269$) and falls to 0.03\%{} under equal wall-clock time ($p=0.8799$), i.e.\ to zero. The batch ran \emph  {before} the two speed-ups of Section\nonbreakingspace \ref {sec:speedup}, so this row is also the motivation for the control below that moves only the speed-ups.}}{39}{figure.caption.39}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:protocols}{{7}{39}{The same set of comparisons under two budget conventions. Each row is named by the mechanism it isolates, not by a pair of configurations. The difference between the two markers comes from the convention: reservation-table-aware dispatching is \GainProbeSameGen {} under equal generation count ($p\PProbeSameGen $) and falls to \GainProbeSameClock {} under equal wall-clock time ($p\PProbeSameClock $), i.e.\ to zero. The batch ran \emph {before} the two speed-ups of Section~\ref {sec:speedup}, so this row is also the motivation for the control below that moves only the speed-ups}{figure.caption.39}{}}
